Since I'm new to the game, I followed the common advice of starting out with 
a Valkyrie, but I can't say I'm in love with this melee-based class.  I 
would like to play a Wizard, but I'm frankly daunted by the NetHack magical 
system (and it's not something I feel confident toying around with using my 
Valk with her INT of 8..."Reading a ...spellbook...with mystic runes beyond 
your ken can be harmful to your health!" - Guidebook).

I know NetHack is somewhat based on AD&D (which I'm familiar with having 
played Baldur's Gate, Icewind Dale, etc.), but it uses Power/Mana/Spell 
Point, Spell Proficiency, and Spell Failure systems which aren't part of 
core AD&D rules (afaik).  The method of learning spells seems to work 
"backwards" from AD&D style (destroying spellbooks to learn a spell and then 
forgetting it the more you use it rather than learning spells from scrolls 
or spellbooks and copying them into your own spellbook permanently).  Spell 
Levels and Spell Types/Categories/Groups are mentioned in the Guidebook, and 
I assume they roughly correspond to Spell Levels and Spell Schools in 
AD&D...but I wouldn't bet my life on it.

Here are some particular questions I have about Spellcasting and Magic in 
Nethack (sort of categorized):

I. SPELL LEVEL
 1.  The Guidebook suggests that there is some correlation between ExpLevel 
and Spell Level.  Is this 1:1, like AD&D (increasing at odd-numbered levels 
iirc), or does it vary depending on INT and/or Class?
 2.  Does your Exp Level have any effect on successfully using magical items 
such as Scrolls or Wands that have the same effect as a Spellbook-Spell? 
(E.g. would a Exp Level 1 character be just as successful as a Exp Level 15 
character when reading a Scroll containing a Spell Level 6 spell?)
 3.  Are there different Spell Level rankings of spells with the same name, 
or do spells get any more potent as the caster's Exp Level increases...or is 
this sort of thing any affected by Spell Proficiency?  (E.g. Do you start 
off learning a weak Level 1 Fireball and then progress up to a potent Level 
5 Fireball, or is Fireball set at say SL4 and gets better as your ExpLevel 
goes up...or is a Fireball a Fireball and the only way to make it better is 
by increasing your Proficiency in its Spell Category/Type/Group?)

II.  SPELL PROFICIENCY / SPELL TYPES
 1.  The Guidebook doesn't mention it, but I suspect that Spell Types are 
linked to character Classes in some manner. (I read somewhere that each 
class as one particular spell that they specialize it, but that might just 
be a rumor.)  After reading the Guidebook my imagination reeled with the 
possibilities of Wizards casting powerful healing spells, Clerics showering 
the battlefield with Magic Missiles, and lowly Tourists summoning forth 
arch-fiends from the nether planes.  But, I doubt the game actually works 
that way or what would be the point of playing one class over another?
 2.  Oh, yeah...my question is this:  Is there a list somewhere of which 
classes can cast which spells or spell types, which spells each class is 
especially good at, and which spells or spell types are forbidden (or just 
*really* difficult) for each class?
 3  How much does Spell Proficiency affect things like Spell Point Cost or 
Spell Failure Chance?



